{info:title=Multi-Team Onboarding with HCP Vault Namespaces}
Comprehensive guide for onboarding multiple teams onto HCP Vault using namespaces with Kubernetes integration and complete isolation.
{info}

{panel:title=Page Information|borderStyle=dashed|borderColor=#ccc|titleBGColor=#d9ead3|bgColor=#f9f9f9}
*Target Audience:* Platform Engineers, Team Leads, Vault Administrators
*Prerequisites:* HCP Vault Plus/Enterprise, Kubernetes cluster admin access
*Related Pages:* [Kubernetes Integration|06-kubernetes-vault-integration], [Advanced Patterns|07-advanced-kubernetes-patterns]
{panel}

h1. Overview

Multi-team onboarding with HCP Vault namespaces provides secure, scalable secret management for organizations with multiple development teams through complete isolation and automated workflows.

{panel:title=Multi-Tenancy Benefits|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#8e44ad|titleBGColor=#9b59b6|titleColor=#ffffff}
• Complete team isolation with Vault namespaces
• Kubernetes namespace mapping for container workloads
• Automated onboarding with consistent configurations
• Granular access control per team and environment
• Centralized governance with distributed management
{panel}

h1. Architecture

h2. Namespace Hierarchy

{noformat}
vault/
├── admin/                     # Root admin namespace
├── platform/                  # Platform engineering team
│   ├── shared/               # Shared platform resources
│   ├── monitoring/           # Monitoring and observability
│   └── infrastructure/       # Infrastructure secrets
├── team-alpha/               # Development team Alpha
│   ├── dev/                  # Development environment
│   ├── staging/              # Staging environment
│   └── prod/                 # Production environment
├── team-beta/                # Development team Beta
├── security/                 # Security team namespace
└── data-engineering/         # Data engineering team
{noformat}

h2. Kubernetes Mapping

{panel:title=Namespace Mapping Strategy|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#3498db|titleBGColor=#2980b9|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Vault Namespace* → *Kubernetes Namespaces*
{{team-alpha}} → {{team-alpha-dev}}, {{team-alpha-staging}}, {{team-alpha-prod}}
{{team-beta}} → {{team-beta-dev}}, {{team-beta-staging}}, {{team-beta-prod}}
{{platform}} → {{platform-monitoring}}, {{platform-infrastructure}}
{panel}

h1. Prerequisites

h2. HCP Vault Requirements

{warning:title=HCP Vault Tier Requirements}
*Vault Namespaces* are only available in HCP Vault *Plus* and *Enterprise* tiers. Standard tier does not support namespaces.
{warning}

{code:language=bash|title=Verify Namespace Support}
# Check if namespaces are supported
vault namespace list

# Verify admin permissions
vault auth list
vault policy list
{code}

h2. Kubernetes Requirements

{code:language=bash|title=Kubernetes Prerequisites}
# Verify cluster admin access
kubectl auth can-i '*' '*' --all-namespaces

# Check Vault Secrets Operator
kubectl get deployment vault-secrets-operator-controller-manager \
    -n vault-secrets-operator-system
{code}

h1. Automated Team Onboarding

h2. Onboarding Script Usage

{code:language=bash|title=Team Onboarding Script}
# Basic team onboarding
./scripts/team-onboarding.sh team-alpha

# Team with custom environments
./scripts/team-onboarding.sh -e "dev,test,staging,prod" team-beta

# Team with cloud engines enabled
./scripts/team-onboarding.sh -c -d team-gamma

# Team with OIDC authentication
./scripts/team-onboarding.sh \
    --oidc \
    --oidc-url "https://auth.company.com/.well-known/openid_configuration" \
    --oidc-client-id "vault-client" \
    --oidc-secret "client-secret" \
    team-delta

# Dry run to preview changes
./scripts/team-onboarding.sh --dry-run team-epsilon
{code}

h2. Script Features

{panel:title=Automation Features|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#27ae60|titleBGColor=#2ecc71|titleColor=#ffffff}
• *Complete Automation:* End-to-end team setup
• *Flexible Configuration:* Custom environments and engines
• *OIDC Integration:* Enterprise SSO authentication
• *Kubernetes Integration:* Automatic namespace and resource creation
• *Documentation Generation:* Automatic team-specific documentation
• *Dry Run Mode:* Preview changes before execution
{panel}

h1. Manual Team Setup

h2. 1. Create Vault Namespace

{code:language=bash|title=Vault Namespace Creation}
# Set admin context
export VAULT_NAMESPACE="admin"

# Create team namespace
vault namespace create team-alpha

# Verify creation
vault namespace list

# Switch to team namespace
export VAULT_NAMESPACE="team-alpha"
{code}

h2. 2. Enable Secrets Engines

{code:language=bash|title=Enable Team Secrets Engines}
# Enable KV v2 secrets engine
vault secrets enable -path=secrets kv-v2

# Enable database secrets engine
vault secrets enable database

# Enable cloud provider engines (optional)
vault secrets enable -path=azure azure
vault secrets enable -path=aws aws
vault secrets enable -path=gcp gcp

# Verify enabled engines
vault secrets list
{code}

h2. 3. Create Team Policies

{expand:title=Development Environment Policy}
{code:language=hcl|title=team-alpha-dev-policy.hcl}
# Full access to dev environment secrets
path "secrets/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

path "secrets/metadata/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list", "delete"]
}

# Read shared configuration
path "secrets/data/shared/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Generate dev database credentials
path "database/creds/dev-*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Generate dev cloud credentials
path "azure/creds/dev-*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
{code}
{expand}

{expand:title=Production Environment Policy}
{code:language=hcl|title=team-alpha-prod-policy.hcl}
# Read-only access to production secrets
path "secrets/data/prod/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Generate production database credentials (read-only)
path "database/creds/prod-readonly" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# Generate production cloud credentials (limited roles)
path "azure/creds/prod-readonly" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
{code}
{expand}

h2. 4. Setup Kubernetes Authentication

{code:language=bash|title=Kubernetes Auth Configuration}
# Enable Kubernetes auth method for team
vault auth enable -path=team-alpha-k8s kubernetes

# Configure Kubernetes auth
vault write auth/team-alpha-k8s/config \
    token_reviewer_jwt="$(kubectl create token vault-auth -n vault-secrets-operator-system --duration=8760h)" \
    kubernetes_host="$(kubectl config view --raw --minify --flatten -o jsonpath='{.clusters[].cluster.server}')" \
    kubernetes_ca_cert="$(kubectl config view --raw --minify --flatten -o jsonpath='{.clusters[].cluster.certificate-authority-data}' | base64 -d)" \
    issuer="https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local"

# Create Kubernetes roles for environments
vault write auth/team-alpha-k8s/role/dev \
    bound_service_account_names="*" \
    bound_service_account_namespaces="team-alpha-dev" \
    policies="team-alpha-dev" \
    ttl=1h \
    max_ttl=4h

vault write auth/team-alpha-k8s/role/prod \
    bound_service_account_names="*" \
    bound_service_account_namespaces="team-alpha-prod" \
    policies="team-alpha-prod" \
    ttl=15m \
    max_ttl=1h
{code}

h1. Kubernetes Integration

h2. Create Team Namespaces

{code:language=bash|title=Kubernetes Namespace Setup}
# Create Kubernetes namespaces for team
kubectl create namespace team-alpha-dev
kubectl create namespace team-alpha-staging
kubectl create namespace team-alpha-prod

# Apply labels for identification
kubectl label namespace team-alpha-dev vault-namespace=team-alpha
kubectl label namespace team-alpha-dev environment=dev
kubectl label namespace team-alpha-dev team=team-alpha

kubectl label namespace team-alpha-prod vault-namespace=team-alpha
kubectl label namespace team-alpha-prod environment=prod
kubectl label namespace team-alpha-prod team=team-alpha
{code}

h2. Deploy Vault Resources

{code:language=yaml|title=VaultConnection for Team}
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultConnection
metadata:
  name: team-alpha-vault
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  address: "https://your-vault-cluster.vault.hashicorp.cloud:8200"
  vaultNamespace: "team-alpha"
  skipTLSVerify: false
---
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultAuth
metadata:
  name: team-alpha-dev-auth
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  vaultConnectionRef: team-alpha-vault
  method: kubernetes
  mount: team-alpha-k8s
  kubernetes:
    role: dev
    serviceAccount: default
---
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultStaticSecret
metadata:
  name: app-config
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  type: kv-v2
  mount: secrets
  path: dev/app/config
  destination:
    name: app-config
    create: true
  vaultAuthRef: team-alpha-dev-auth
{code}

h1. Team Access Management

h2. OIDC Authentication

{code:language=bash|title=OIDC Setup for Teams}
# Enable OIDC auth for team
vault auth enable -path=team-alpha-oidc oidc -namespace=team-alpha

# Configure OIDC
vault write auth/team-alpha-oidc/config \
    oidc_discovery_url="https://auth.company.com/.well-known/openid_configuration" \
    oidc_client_id="team-alpha-vault-client" \
    oidc_client_secret="client-secret" \
    default_role="team-alpha-member"

# Create OIDC roles
vault write auth/team-alpha-oidc/role/team-alpha-admin \
    bound_audiences="team-alpha-vault-client" \
    user_claim="sub" \
    policies="team-alpha-admin"

vault write auth/team-alpha-oidc/role/team-alpha-developer \
    bound_audiences="team-alpha-vault-client" \
    user_claim="sub" \
    policies="team-alpha-dev"
{code}

h2. User Management

{code:language=bash|title=Team User Management}
# Create team admin policy
cat > team-alpha-admin.hcl << 'EOF'
# Full access to team namespace
path "secrets/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

# Manage team policies
path "sys/policies/acl/team-alpha-*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

# View team audit logs
path "sys/audit" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}
EOF

vault policy write team-alpha-admin team-alpha-admin.hcl -namespace=team-alpha
{code}

h1. Cross-Team Patterns

h2. Shared Resources

{code:language=bash|title=Shared Platform Resources}
# Create shared namespace for platform resources
vault namespace create shared

# Switch to shared namespace
export VAULT_NAMESPACE="shared"

# Enable KV engine for shared resources
vault secrets enable -path=secrets kv-v2

# Create shared readonly policy
cat > shared-readonly.hcl << 'EOF'
path "secrets/data/common/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

path "secrets/data/platform/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF

vault policy write shared-readonly shared-readonly.hcl

# Grant access from team namespaces
vault write auth/team-alpha-k8s/role/shared \
    bound_service_account_namespaces="team-alpha-dev,team-alpha-prod" \
    policies="shared-readonly" \
    -namespace=shared
{code}

h2. Cross-Namespace Secret Access

{code:language=yaml|title=Cross-Namespace VaultStaticSecret}
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultConnection
metadata:
  name: shared-vault
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  address: "https://your-vault-cluster.vault.hashicorp.cloud:8200"
  vaultNamespace: "shared"
  skipTLSVerify: false
---
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultAuth
metadata:
  name: shared-auth
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  vaultConnectionRef: shared-vault
  method: kubernetes
  mount: kubernetes
  kubernetes:
    role: shared
    serviceAccount: default
---
apiVersion: secrets.hashicorp.com/v1beta1
kind: VaultStaticSecret
metadata:
  name: platform-config
  namespace: team-alpha-dev
spec:
  type: kv-v2
  mount: secrets
  path: platform/monitoring
  destination:
    name: platform-config
    create: true
  vaultAuthRef: shared-auth
{code}

h1. Governance and Monitoring

h2. Namespace Audit Configuration

{code:language=bash|title=Per-Namespace Audit Logging}
# Enable audit logging per namespace
vault audit enable -path=team-alpha-audit file \
    file_path=/vault/audit/team-alpha.log \
    -namespace=team-alpha

# Configure audit log rotation
vault write sys/config/auditing/request-headers \
    X-Forwarded-For=true \
    X-Real-IP=true \
    -namespace=team-alpha
{code}

h2. Cross-Namespace Monitoring

{code:language=bash|title=Platform Admin Monitoring}
# Policy for platform administrators (admin namespace)
cat > platform-admin.hcl << 'EOF'
# Manage all namespaces
path "sys/namespaces/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list"]
}

# Read audit logs from all namespaces
path "+/sys/audit" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}

# Monitor namespace health
path "+/sys/health" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}

# View all namespace policies
path "+/sys/policies/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}
EOF

vault policy write platform-admin platform-admin.hcl -namespace=admin
{code}

h1. Best Practices

{panel:title=Multi-Team Best Practices|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#e67e22|titleBGColor=#f39c12|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Namespace Design:*
• Use consistent naming: {{team-<name>}}
• Plan for future team reorganization
• Separate production from non-production clearly

*Access Control:*
• Implement principle of least privilege
• Use time-bound tokens with appropriate TTLs
• Regular access reviews per team

*Operations:*
• Automate team onboarding processes
• Monitor namespace usage and performance
• Implement backup procedures per namespace
{panel}

h1. Troubleshooting

h2. Common Issues

{expand:title=Namespace Access Problems}
*Symptoms:* Team members cannot access their namespace

*Solutions:*
1. Verify namespace exists: {{vault namespace list}}
2. Check namespace context: {{echo $VAULT_NAMESPACE}}
3. Verify user authentication and policies
4. Test namespace access: {{vault auth -namespace=team-alpha}}
{expand}

{expand:title=Cross-Namespace Secret Access}
*Symptoms:* Cannot access shared secrets from team namespace

*Solutions:*
1. Verify shared namespace authentication
2. Check cross-namespace policies
3. Validate VaultConnection configuration
4. Test shared secret access manually
{expand}

